WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.657
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.657 --> 00:00:09.950
What do you think of when you hear
the words, violence prevention?

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.530
Seriously, take a second and
think about that.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.530
Many of you are probably
thinking law enforcement, right?

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:24.720
Well, my hope is that by the end of this
talk, healthcare comes into your mind

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.320
just as quickly as law enforcement
probably did, if not even faster.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:36.230
So one question that some folks often
ask is, why is violence so common?

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.580
Violence is a pretty widely
agreed upon societal vice.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.770
We all know it's bad and
yet, it's everywhere.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:49.200
According to the FBI's Uniform
Crime Reporting Program, in 2016,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.015
there were over 1.2 million violent
crimes committed in the US.

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:56.820
Over 17,000 of these resulted in deaths.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.550
Now that's the size of the entire
student population here at Stanford.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.760
So it's a fair question.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.530
Why is violence so common?

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.280
Let me try and
explain with a personal story.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.410
When I was 12, I moved into the Sumner
Housing Projects of Brooklyn, New York.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.330
And I was actually really excited about
this because there was a basketball court,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.970
right in front of my new apartment.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.120
I've been playing basketball for
a while and still wasn't very good but

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.040
you see all that would change now
because I could practice on this court.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.130
I just remember going up
to all my friends and

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.750
saying, hey guys, give me about a year
practice and I am dunking on everybody.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.690
I was pretty delusional back then, yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:37.640
&gt;&gt; So one day I was out there on

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.500
the court, shooting around and
as I said, I was not very good, so

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.160
most of those shots missed.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.370
And on one of the misses
the ball bounced off the rim and

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.030
rolled to the side of the park.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.000
Another kid picks it up and walks off.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.560
So I run up to him and say,
hey man, hey, that's my ball!

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.550
And he keeps on walking.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.780
And before I know it,
I'm surrounded by about 20 other kids,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:06.370
all with their fists clenched and
eyebrows furrowed.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.170
And they were yelling, yo, yo.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:10.930
What you doing here, Sam?

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.310
What you doing here?

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:12.810
Who you with?

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.870
I was a fresh-faced,
clueless 12 year old kid.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:17.620
I didn't know what to say.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.220
I got beat up pretty badly that day.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.480
And as I eventually managed to push my way
out of the crowd of sneakers and fists and

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.270
ran home eyes full of tears, I thought
the next time something like this happens,

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.080
I need to have something to say.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.540
Who am I with, who can defend me here and
that's how you end up befriending some

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.800
pretty sketchy characters that introduce
you to gangs, guns and before you know it,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.580
you've been fully initiated into
the vicious cycle of violence.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.450
I was lucky in that I
have an incredible mom.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.477
Whenever I screwed up,
she would put me in my place.

00:02:57.477 --> 00:03:00.290
It's funny really,
I'm a lot taller than my mom.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:00.860
It didn't matter.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.140
She would stand up on top of a chair if
she had to, look down at me screaming at

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.060
the top of her lungs to make sure that
I stayed on the straight and narrow.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.060
But unfortunately, there are a lot of
other folks in these kinds of communities

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.790
that don't have that influence and thus
end up with this huge issue of violence.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.260
Now I want you all to imagine yourselves
growing up in Sumner right along with me.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.350
To help you do that,
let's paint a picture.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.990
Sumner is actually a really
lively neighborhood.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.520
There are tons of beautiful parks and

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.330
trees, little kids running around as
they play on pretty much every block.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.760
Now, there is

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.470
loud music always blasting from
someone's window behind you, barbecues.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.880
You're surrounded by
a whole lot of happiness.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.590
But also, a whole lot of violence.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:52.240
Growing up in Sumner, you saw gangs,
drugs, fighting, shooting,

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.430
constant policing, the whole night.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:04:00.300
Now, imagine the inevitable happens and

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.157
[NOISE] you get shot.

00:04:03.157 --> 00:04:04.410
What happens next?

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.060
Someone calls 911.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.140
An ambulance rushes down, picks you up and

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.050
brings you to the nearest
emergency department.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.786
There, you're treated, stabilized and

00:04:11.786 --> 00:04:14.754
thankfully recovering
in your hospital bed.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:17.140
What do you see around you?

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.510
All kinds of tubes sticking in and

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.640
out of your body, loud beeping
sounds that never seem to stop.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.090
Unfamiliar faces in these bright,
white coats coming in and out and in and

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:27.920
out of your room.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.430
What's going through
your mind in this moment?

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:36.000
Probably something like,
wow, I almost died today.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:43.450
I may have never seen my parents,
siblings, boyfriend, girlfriend, anyone.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:48.464
Again, I can't go back to this life,

00:04:48.464 --> 00:04:51.780
someone, anyone help.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.550
And in that moment,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.370
you are probably more receptive to
intervention that you've ever been before.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:03.010
But that is also a huge window of
opportunity that law enforcement just

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.080
doesn't have but unfortunately in many
incidences these patients are treated and

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.350
then send right back to the same violent
environment that spurn those injuries in

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.550
the first place with no means
of preventing recurrence.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.950
So you may be asking okay but
what can we actually do for

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:20.140
these folks inside of a hospital?

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.390
Well, a lot actually.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.640
I'm here at Standford for an MBA but
I'm also a fourth year medical student

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.390
at Harvard, while there, I worked
with the Violence Recovery Program or

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.540
the VRP at the Brigham and
Women's Hospital.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.770
The VRP is a hospital based violence
prevention program that targets patients

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.530
who are victims and perpetrators
of violence and provides them with

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:46.820
resources necessary to stop them
from coming back to the hospital.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:51.260
Now, at the VRP, this mainly took
the form of counseling but our counselors

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:55.510
are unique in that they each have their
own personal experiences with violence.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:58.590
So often, let me add that that was

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.900
In the very same neighborhoods
that these patients were from.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.890
So where as more traditional counselors
might have been trained at a graduate

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:11.370
program, these folks are trained by
life and that enabled them to establish

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:16.480
a much more personal connection with our
patients but it doesn't need to end there.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.010
One can imagine, also including things
like housing relocation, job training,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.560
legal support, educational support,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:29.050
really any number of services that would
be the most effective for a given patient.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.840
And the beautiful thing is that these
services often already exist in

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.800
the communities that
these patients are from.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.780
And thus all a hospital based program
would need to do, would be to connect

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:44.350
these patients to these services
during this window of opportunity.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:48.452
Imagine the impact this
could have is skilled.

00:06:48.452 --> 00:06:52.596
Now, there are a number of similar
programs across the country and

00:06:52.596 --> 00:06:58.072
they're all housed under a larger umbrella
organization known as the National Network

00:06:58.072 --> 00:07:03.280
for Hospital-based Violence
Intervention Programs or the NNHVIP.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.541
There are about 32 programs
within this network and

00:07:06.541 --> 00:07:10.322
they are all doing great work but
again, just 32 programs.

00:07:10.322 --> 00:07:13.442
There are far more than 32
hospitals in this country and

00:07:13.442 --> 00:07:16.370
even more communities that
struggle with violence.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:22.020
Violence is a tremendous issue,
according to the CDC, violence is listed

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.410
as the second and third leading
causes of death among American youth.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.620
Second being suicide and third, homicide.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.090
But that places it higher than any of
the more traditional medical disorders

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.010
you can probably think of.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.580
Cancer, heart disease,
diabetes, all of that.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:44.210
So, for something that has such
a large impact on the health and

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:45.710
mortality of patients.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:49.270
I find it imperative that we
in healthcare address this.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:54.420
So, I want to ask you all
to consider three things.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:56.420
And before I say that,

00:07:56.420 --> 00:08:01.110
I just want to stress this point that
violence is a public health issue and

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:05.620
we can't leave it to law
enforcement to deal with all alone.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.380
So for those three things.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:12.960
One, if you're a clinician
in the audience or

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.720
watching online, if you work in a hospital
that serves communities struggling with

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.430
violence, consider starting your
own violence prevention program.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:24.999
It could go a long way in improving the
overall health of your patient population.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:30.630
Two, if you're not a clinician,
maybe you know one or

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:35.730
a hospital administrator, a policy maker,
really any decision maker in this space.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:39.200
Use those connections and
convince those folks to try and

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.370
help create more of these programs.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:46.320
And three, if you're not a clinician and
don't know any decision makers,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.800
that's okay too.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:51.760
At the very least, think back to the
question that we started this talk with.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.120
What do you think of when you hear
the words violence prevention?

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.120
I hope that now,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.780
you can appreciate the impact that
healthcare can have in reducing violence.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.930
And the next time you talk
about violence prevention,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:08.386
I hope that healthcare is one of the first
things that comes into your mind.

00:09:08.386 --> 00:09:15.432
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:15.432 --> 00:09:19.112
[MUSIC]

